   "newText": "This illustrates: enumerate the full grid carefully—there can be matches far from the template, and missing even one causes rejection. The demo for the other case in this file used a different mask; infer template shape from each input instead of conflating these variants.\n\n## Additional solved instance: task-3caaa5de (2026-10-20, 16x18)\nCorrectly solved on the first submission after requesting two demos. In the test, the background is 0, blockers are 3, and the seed/template color is 2. The seed occupies rows 9–11, columns 10–13 (0-indexed bounding box 3x4), with exact mask offsets from its top-left:\n`[(0,0),(0,2),(0,3),(1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2)]`.\nRule: preserve the grid and seed; for every in-bounds translation of this fixed-orientation mask where all mask cells are 0, change precisely those cells to 2. Do not rotate/reflect the mask or change non-mask cells. Exhaustively scan placements across the entire grid. For this 16x18 test there were 22 matching top-lefts (0-indexed): `(0,7),(0,9),(0,12),(1,3),(1,10),(1,11),(1,12),(3,1),(3,13),(4,2),(5,8),(7,5),(8,0),(8,1),(9,0),(9,1),(11,5),(12,6),(12,7),(12,8),(12,9),(13,6)`. Overlaying all these masks with color 2 and preserving everything else was confirmed correct.\n\nThe two demos reinforce the general rule despite different colors and different template shapes per instance: copy the seed's exact occupied-cell mask into every translated all-background match. Demo 2 has color 3 mask `[(0,1),(1,0),(1,1),(1,2),(2,2)]` in a 3x4 box (background 0, blockers 6). Demo 1 has color 4 and likewise scattered exact-mask copies in background-0 holes among 5s. Always derive the mask afresh from the current seed; do not reuse another instance's mask. A small script that checks all offsets and recolors only matched mask positions is reliable."
